# Next.js Rules

## Server Components (Default)

- All components are Server Components unless they need interactivity.
- Fetch data directly in Server Components via application services.
- Pass serializable props to Client Components.
- No `useState`, `useEffect`, event handlers, or browser APIs in Server Components.

## Client Components

- Add `'use client'` ONLY when the component uses: `useState`, `useEffect`, `useRef`, event handlers, browser APIs, or shadcn/ui interactive primitives.
- Keep Client Components **small and leaf-level**.
- NEVER add `'use client'` to layouts or high-level page components.
- Do NOT make a parent Client Component just because a child needs interactivity — push the boundary down.

## Server Actions

- Use for all mutations (create, update, delete).
- Define in `src/features/[feature]/actions/`.
- Mark with `'use server'`.
- Validate input with Zod at the action boundary.
- Delegate to Application use cases. Do NOT put business logic in actions.
- Return `Result<T, E>`.
- Call `revalidatePath()` or `revalidateTag()` after success.

## Route Handlers

- Use ONLY for: webhooks, external API callbacks, file uploads, SSE.
- Do NOT use for standard CRUD — use Server Actions.

## Data Fetching

- Server Components fetch data with `async/await`. No `useEffect` fetching.
- Use React `cache()` for request-level deduplication.
- Use `revalidateTag()` for cache invalidation after mutations.

## File Conventions

- `layout.tsx` — Shared UI. Keep thin. Never `'use client'`.
- `page.tsx` — Route entry. Compose features. Fetch data.
- `loading.tsx` — Suspense fallback (skeleton/spinner).
- `error.tsx` — Error boundary. Must be Client Component. Log errors.
- `not-found.tsx` — 404 page. Trigger with `notFound()`.

## Caching

| Strategy | Use |
|----------|-----|
| `cache()` (React) | Deduplicate within a request |
| `revalidatePath()` | Invalidate specific route |
| `revalidateTag()` | Invalidate by tag |
| `export const revalidate = N` | Time-based revalidation |

See `docs/coding-conventions.md` for full details.